%I A401006 #22 Oct 13 2026 18:23:19
%S A401006 1,2,3,22,5,23,7,42,33,25,11,43,13,27,35,82,17,63,19,45,37,211,23,83,
%T A401006 55,213,93,47,29,65,31,162,311,217,57,123,37,219,313,85,41,67,43,411,
%U A401006 95,223,47,163,77,105,317,413,53,183,511,87,319,229,59,125,61,231,97,322,513,611
%N A401006 a(1)=1; for n >= 2, a(n) = n if n is prime; otherwise, a(n) is the decimal concatenation of n/p followed by p, where p is the largest prime factor of n.
%C A401006 Conjecture: if we start at n >= 2, and repeatedly apply the map k -> a(k), we will eventually reach a prime, which is then a fixed point. For composite n = m*p, where p is the largest prime factor of n, and d is the number of  digits in p, a(n) - n = m*(10^d - p) + p > 0, so no cycle of composite numbers can occur.
%H A401006 Derek Steinkamp, <a href="/A401006/b401006.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401006 Nicholas Zoller, <a href="https://pillars.taylor.edu/acms-2015/23/">Home Primes and Foreign Primes</a>, ACMS Conference Proceedings 2015.
%F A401006 For composite n, a(n) = A052126(n)*10^A055642(A006530(n)) + A006530(n).
%F A401006 For n = 1 or prime n, a(n) = n.
%e A401006 a(8) = 42 because 8 = 4*2 and 2 is its largest prime factor. a(12) = 43 because 12 = 4*3. a(44) = 411 because 44 = 4*11. Iteration from 49 gives 49 -> 77 -> 711 -> 979 -> 1189 -> 2941 -> 17173 -> 131321, and 131321 is prime.
%t A401006 FoldList[If[PrimeQ[#2], #2, With[{p = FactorInteger[#2][[-1, 1]]}, 10^IntegerLength[p]*#2/p + p]] &, Range[100]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 13 2026 *)
%o A401006 (Python)
%o A401006 """Minimal standard-library program for the proposed OEIS entry."""
%o A401006 def largest_prime_factor(n):
%o A401006     largest = 1
%o A401006     divisor = 2
%o A401006     while divisor * divisor <= n:
%o A401006         while n % divisor == 0:
%o A401006             largest = divisor
%o A401006             n //= divisor
%o A401006         divisor += 1
%o A401006     return max(largest, n)
%o A401006 def a(n):
%o A401006     if n == 1:
%o A401006         return 1
%o A401006     p = largest_prime_factor(n)
%o A401006     if p == n:
%o A401006         return n
%o A401006     q = n // p
%o A401006     return q * 10 ** len(str(p)) + p
%o A401006 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 101)])
%o A401006 (PARI) a(n) = if (n==1, 1, if (isprime(n), n,  my(p=vecmax(factor(n)[,1])); eval(concat(Str(n/p), Str(p))))); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Oct 13 2026
